<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hawaiian sovereignty movement</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ke ea Hawaiʻi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hawaiian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> movement (ke ea Hawaiʻi) is a grassroots political and cultural campaign to re-establish an autonomous or independent Hawaiian nation or kingdom. It generally holds both the overthrow of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lili-uokalani">Liliʻuokalani</a> in 1893 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>' annexation of 1898 to have been illegal, and some groups seek redress. Led by Native Hawaiian organisations and activists since the 1960s and 1970s, it has opposed commercial development, the appropriation of burial grounds and sacred places and the bombing of Kahoʻolawe. In 1993 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> passed the Apology Resolution, acknowledging that the overthrow of the Hawaiian Kingdom was illegal.</p></div>
